WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
Change review starts with scope.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:13.160
Teams need to understand the cloud service offering, the authorization boundary, included and excluded systems, inherited services, customer responsibilities, and supporting dependencies.

3
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:20.220
A change that affects an in-scope production component may require a different level of review than a change to an unrelated internal tool.

4
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:27.760
Boundary awareness helps the team decide which stakeholders, evidence, and risk questions are relevant.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.840
Security-relevant changes can take many forms.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:38.380
Infrastructure changes may alter compute, storage, network paths, encryption settings, or deployment automation.

7
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:47.360
Application changes may affect authentication, authorization, data handling, APIs, logging, or customer workflows.

8
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.700
Identity and access changes may create or remove privileges.

9
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.000
Network changes may expose or isolate services.

10
00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.380
Data flow changes may alter where information is processed, stored, or transmitted.

11
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.300
Other change types deserve attention as well.

12
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.640
Logging and monitoring changes can affect detection and evidence.

13
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.940
Security tool changes can alter control operation.

14
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.400
Vendor or dependency changes can shift inherited responsibilities or introduce new risk.

15
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:25.460
Emergency changes may be necessary during outages or security events, but urgency does not remove the need for traceability, validation, and follow-up.

16
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.040
Unplanned does not have to mean unmanaged.

17
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.680
The central question is whether a change affects the authorized or FedRAMP-aligned system.

18
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.100
Does it alter components in scope?

19
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.280
Does it change a control implementation?

20
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.740
Does it affect inherited controls or customer responsibility?

21
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:48.000
Does it alter data flows, administrative interfaces, evidence, or monitoring coverage?

22
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:54.580
A clear answer helps the organization avoid both over-processing routine work and under-reviewing meaningful risk.
